bird-manager for Debian
========================

bird-manager is a Web dashboard for BIRD network, ASN:COMM and BGP peer
management. SQLite is the only and enforced data source for the BIRD daemon.
The service renders split BIRD configuration files into staging and publishes
them only after an explicit Apply.

See the full manual: `man bird-manager`.

Installation layout
-------------------

  /usr/lib/bird-manager             application code
  /etc/bird-manager/.env            runtime configuration (conffile)
  /var/lib/bird-manager/app.db      SQLite database
  /var/lib/bird-manager/staged      generated staging files
  /var/lib/bird-manager/backup      full snapshots
  /etc/bird/live                    live files shared with BIRD
  /etc/bird/bird.conf               operator config plus stable manager include

The service runs as the dedicated `bird-manager` system user, supplementary
group `bird`, and can write only `/etc/bird/live` and `/var/lib/bird-manager`.
The live directory is setgid, so atomic replacements inherit group `bird` for
BIRD reads. The main BIRD configuration remains read-only to the service. The project uses
system Python packages and does not ship a virtualenv. BIRD (`bird3`, `bird2` or
`bird`) must be installed.

Generated files
---------------

  /etc/bird/live/bird-manager.conf  last applied ordered include list
  /etc/bird/live/networks.conf       last applied editable base and router id
  /etc/bird/live/routes.conf         last applied generated static routes
  /etc/bird/live/core.conf           last applied device/direct/BGP protocols
  /etc/bird/live/policy_<id>.conf    last applied community policy
  /var/lib/bird-manager/staged/      current staged candidate

Only `networks.conf` is exposed in the web editor. Database mutations update
staged files; the live bundle and BIRD are changed only after Apply. Generated
files are not manually editable. In a reset installation `networks.conf`
contains only the generated header and router id.

Connecting to BIRD
------------------

bird-manager keeps one stable marked block in `/etc/bird/bird.conf`:

    include "/etc/bird/live/bird-manager.conf";

The generated entrypoint uses the normal order:

    include "/etc/bird/live/networks.conf";
    include "/etc/bird/live/policy_<id>.conf";
    include "/etc/bird/live/routes.conf";
    include "/etc/bird/live/core.conf";

Apply never writes the main config. The service validates the complete bundle
with `bird -p` and reloads BIRD with unprivileged `birdc configure`.

For a manual BIRD setup:

    install bird3            # or bird2
    systemctl enable --now bird
    birdc show status

If `/etc/bird/bird.conf` is an external configuration, the package asks
whether to append the stable include. Existing content is preserved and a
pre-migration backup is kept. If the answer is `No`, runtime Apply remains
disabled until an administrator completes the migration.

BGP peers
---------

BGP peers are managed through the web panel and API. Each peer has an identifier
name, local ASN, neighbor IPv4 and neighbor ASN. Peer reachability routes are
generated in `routes.conf`; the BGP protocol itself is generated in `core.conf`.

Database behavior
-----------------

`Network.enabled` and `Community.enabled` are JSON booleans. Disabled networks
are omitted from routes and policy filters. A community with no enabled
networks is automatically disabled. Deleting a community deletes all related
networks and generated policy content.

The Web API has no authentication. The optional `user_id` is recorded in the
audit log. A failed BIRD reload does not stop the web service: `/api/health`
returns `degraded` with `reload_warning` and `reload_detail`.

First-time checks
-----------------

    curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/api/health
    birdc show status
    systemctl status bird-manager

On a fresh install, the package creates a minimal `networks.conf`, starts the
service, and leaves the generated bundle in staging until the first Apply.

Environment overrides
---------------------

Edit `/etc/bird-manager/.env` to change paths, router id, local ASN or binary
paths. Package defaults include:

    BIRD_LIVE_DIR=/etc/bird/live
    BIRD_STAGED_DIR=/var/lib/bird-manager/staged
    BIRD_BACKUP_RETENTION=5
    USERNAME=User
    DB_PATH=/var/lib/bird-manager/app.db

Restart the service after editing:

    systemctl restart bird-manager

`USERNAME` is the identifier written to journal and audit entries. It does not
grant permissions or access. The default is `User`.

Access control
--------------

The JSON API has no authentication, so the source allowlist is the only gate in
front of the BIRD configuration. It is checked before routing and before the
request body is read.

    BIRD_TRUSTED_SOURCES=auto,127.0.0.1/32,::1/128
    BIRD_TRUSTED_PROXIES=
    BIRD_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=
    BIRD_MAX_BODY_BYTES=1048576
    BIRD_DEBUG_ERRORS=0
    APP_HOST=0.0.0.0

- `BIRD_TRUSTED_SOURCES` is a comma separated CIDR list. `auto` resolves to the
  network of this host's primary IPv4 interface, so the panel is reachable from
  the local network out of the box. Docker and VPN bridge subnets are never
  added automatically; add them explicitly if a container must reach the panel.
- Requests outside the list are answered with `403` and the reason is written to
  the journal together with the client address.
- `BIRD_TRUSTED_PROXIES` lists reverse proxies allowed to set
  `X-Forwarded-For`. While it is empty the header is ignored completely, so a
  client cannot fake its address.
- `BIRD_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` lists origins allowed to send `POST`/`PUT`/`DELETE`.
  When it is set, a mutating request with a missing or foreign `Origin` gets
  `403`, which blocks cross-site requests from a browser on the same network.
  `curl` and scripts send no `Origin`, so either add their origin or leave the
  variable empty.
- Request bodies larger than `BIRD_MAX_BODY_BYTES` get `413`.
- Every response carries `X-Content-Type-Options`, `X-Frame-Options`,
  `Referrer-Policy`, a strict `Content-Security-Policy` and
  `Cache-Control: no-store`. 5xx responses return a generic message; the
  traceback stays in the journal unless `BIRD_DEBUG_ERRORS=1`.
- The effective policy is printed at service start, so `journalctl -u
  bird-manager` always shows the current allowlist.

On upgrade the conffile keeps the operator's values; missing keys are reported
in the install log and the same safe built-in defaults apply.

Example for nginx as the only entry point:

    APP_HOST=127.0.0.1
    BIRD_TRUSTED_SOURCES=auto,127.0.0.1/32
    BIRD_TRUSTED_PROXIES=127.0.0.1/32
    BIRD_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://bird.example.net

with

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:5000;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    }

All timestamps (journal, audit, backup list, status) use the server machine's
local timezone. Rollback snapshots are kept in `/var/lib/bird-manager/live-bak`,
so the BIRD include directory stays free of `*.bak` files.

Backup and restore
------------------

The Web backup endpoint stores SQLite, staged/live files and the generated-file
manifest. It keeps a local rotating history controlled by
`BIRD_BACKUP_RETENTION` (default `5`, valid range `1..99`). The System tab
shows used/limit slots and one row per archive with restore and delete actions.
Selected restores are validated with `bird -p` before state is replaced and
followed by `birdc configure`. The main config is kept in the archive for
offline recovery but is never restored over the current installation. Legacy
archives without `bird-manager.conf` synthesize the entrypoint during restore.

Uninstall
---------

`apt remove bird-manager` asks whether to remove all application data. The
default is **No**, which keeps the database, backups, environment file and live
configuration for a later reinstall.

Choosing **Yes** removes:

- `/var/lib/bird-manager` (SQLite, staged files, backups and manifests);
- `/etc/bird-manager/.env` and the package-owned system account;
- package-created files in `/etc/bird/live` while preserving unknown files.

Before live files are removed, the managed block in `/etc/bird/bird.conf` is
replaced inline with the current effective BIRD configuration. The candidate
must pass `bird -p`; when BIRD is active, `birdc configure` must also succeed.
On failure the original main file is restored in memory and cleanup is stopped.
No persistent backup copy of `bird.conf` is created.

`apt purge bird-manager` performs the same full cleanup without asking again.
Neither command removes the BIRD package, its service or the `bird` group.
